00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
for working parents and carers managing  family life and professional career is  often a challenge especially for women  today women are still more likely to  take up caring responsibilities than men  this is one of the reasons why women are  still underrepresented on the labour  market although there represents 60% of  university graduates thanks to the  work/life balance directive the EU  introduces new minimum rights and  extends existing ones for working  parents and carers so that there's a  better sharing of care and  responsibilities within families the  work-life balance directive provides for  at least 10 working days of paternity  leave when a child is born paid sick pay  level at least four months of parental  leave two of those four months cannot be  transferred between parents and are  adequately paid at least five days of  leave per year for taking care of a  relative in need of significant care or  support the right to request flexible  working arrangements for all carers and  parents of children up to the age of at  least eight years these work-life  balance rules aim to offer both women  and men a real choice in finding the  right balance between their work and  family life this is fair and good for  families businesses member states and  the economy as a whole  [Music]
 

